European natural gas prices have fallen  for the first time below their values  before the war in Ukraine to in large  part to lower demands and improved gas  and electricity Supply in the near term  the price for a month ahead contract at  Europe's large largest Exchange in  Amsterdam was trading around 83 Euros  per megawatt hour on Thursday prices  spiked in August more than 300 Euros as  markets registered a supply shock that's  when Russia began curtailing pipeline  deliveries  Olivia Galleria is a senior Analyst at  energy aspects she joins me for more  from London Olivia welcome to the show I  want to start with gas prices what does  this mean for households and businesses  in Europe is the gas crisis in Germany  for example now ending or is an end in  sight  thanks for having me so European gas  prices have indeed Fallen sharply over  the last couple of months thanks to what  I would say a lucky combination of  factors for Europe including  unseasonably mild weather  um limit competition from China which  have helped here build stocks and  therefore have dragged prices lower  however this does not mean we should get  complacent here to be honest there are  still a lot of factors that could lead  to tight markets next winter in  particular  um and that might mean prices rise back  up again for example China is coming  back to the market with the covet  situation easing over there  industrial demand could be coming back  as well in Europe now the prices are a  little bit lower we can't rule out a  cold spell  um in the winter you know in the next  couple of months and also Europe will  need to balance next winter with a lot  less Russian gas than ever did before so  all of this could mean we're still  facing a very hard situation and even in  the longer term we also need to consider  what high prices will do to the European  industry which is something that will  have to continue to face for many years  to come  I want to ask you about another  commodity oil Vladimir Putin announcing  this week that Russia will end oil sales  to buyers who observe the price cap put  in place by the EU G7 and observed by  some Nations like Australia not exactly  a surprise but what if anything does  that mean for the country's oil revenues  will it suffer significantly because of  this or is it just sort of moot  yeah  prices are indeed  um  lower  but you know by historical standards  they are not extremely low and with for  example the price cap that has been  agreed by the G7 that you mentioned at  60 dollars per barrel that's just below  what Russian government budget have been  set for around 70 dollars per barrel for  2023 so we're not very far off from  those levels so I would say overall  prices at the moment for Russia are not  that big of a concern I think the  biggest factor to consider at the moment  is how much oil and gas is Russia able  to sell  um and to export and therefore how that  is impacting overall revenues  um if you consider for example the  European embargo on Russian oil  um with that Russia has lost its biggest  market for oil and we and many other  analysts don't think that Russia will be  able to shift all the oil that it used  to import to Europe to Asia for example  and that means losses in exports and  production and therefore revenues and  it's the same situation with gas if  anything even more so because on the gas  side you actually have some significant  infrastructural constraints that mean  that Russia cannot divert as much gas  that is used to export to Europe via  pipeline or lmg to Asia  this price cap was based on the concept  or on the idea the supposition I suppose  that Russia would not cease production  or slow down production that it could  not afford to do that is that true that  it essentially has to continue producing  at the same levels and then essentially  find ways to divert it across the global  economy  we are not seeing that happening in fact  Russian gas and oil production has  already fallen uh by uh by quite a lot  since the start of the war and we expect  it to fall even further uh in the coming  months as it loses the European market  in particular  um the problem is that once that  production is shut in it's very  difficult for Russia to get it back  because of the nature of its oil  reserves which are highly depleted old  oil fields that cannot be switched on  and off like a light switch  all right that's Livia galarati at  energy aspects thank you very much
 

